        "cry came up to God",
        "word",
        "Hebrew",
        "Hebrew",
        "<strong>Tza‘aqah (<em>צְעָקָה</em>) — “outcry.”</strong> The same noun used for Sodom’s cry (Gen 18:20–21) and the cry of the oppressed throughout the Hebrew Bible. It triggers the four-verb sequence that follows in 2:24–25 — God <em>heard, remembered, saw, knew</em> — each rendered active and concrete. In Hebrew idiom, divine “remembering” (<em>zakhar</em>) is never mental recall but always the prelude to intervention; the cry inaugurates the liberation that the rest of the book unfolds.",
        "User original",
