        "Hebrew",
        "Hebrew",
        "<strong>S’neh (<em>סְנֶה</em>) — “bramble.”</strong> The unusual word for the burning shrub (used in only two passages of the Bible, both Mosaic) is widely thought to pun on Sinai (<em>Sinai</em> / <em>S’neh</em>) — the mountain of revelation embedded already in the bush of revelation. Rabbinic tradition asks why God appears in a thornbush and not a great cedar; one answer (<em>Shemot Rabbah</em> 2:5) is that no place is too low for the divine presence.",
        "User original",
