        "",
        "comm-ethiopian",
        "Tewahedo — Ephrem the Syrian",
        "Ephrem the Syrian (360).",
        '<aside class="note-comm-ethiopian"><strong>Ephrem the Syrian</strong> <em>Commentary on Exodus</em> <small>(c. 360 AD)</small><p>Moses&#x27; hand made leprous in the bosom and then restored is, for Ephrem, the sign of the Incarnation given before the fact: humanity has become diseased in its hidden parts, and the Lord places it within his own bosom to bring it forth healed. The same hand that becomes leprous becomes healing for the leprous — a foreshadowing of the Word&#x27;s assumption of the very flesh he will cleanse.</p></aside>',
        "Ephrem the Syrian, Commentary on Exodus IV.3 (NPNF S2 V13, Gwynn/Schaff trans., Oxford 1898). PD.",
