        "Ephrem the Syrian (360).",
        '<aside class="note-comm-ethiopian"><strong>Ephrem the Syrian</strong> <em>Commentary on Exodus</em> <small>(c. 360 AD)</small><p>&#x27;I will take you to me for a people, and I will be to you a God&#x27; — Ephrem hears in this verse the completion of the Abrahamic-Isaac-Jacob promise: the patriarchal seed becomes a peoplehood under a single covenanted Name. Tewahedo cites Ex 6:7 as the canonical formula on which both Sinai and the New-Covenant &#x27;I will be their God and they shall be my people&#x27; (Jer 31:33; Rev 21:3) depend.</p></aside>',
