        "comm-ethiopian",
        "Tewahedo — Athanasius of Alexandria",
        "Athanasius of Alexandria (350).",
        '<aside class="note-comm-ethiopian"><strong>Athanasius of Alexandria</strong> <em>Orationes contra Arianos III.19</em> <small>(c. 350 AD)</small><p>&#x27;And the Lord said unto Moses, See, I have made thee a god to Pharaoh: and Aaron thy brother shall be thy prophet&#x27; — Athanasius marks dedōka se theon tō Pharaō (I have given thee as a god to Pharaoh) as the METHODOLOGICAL-KEY for the gods-by-participation vs theotēs-by-nature distinction. Moses is called &#x27;god&#x27; by ECONOMIC-COMMUNICATION (his role-as-divine-mediator to Pharaoh) and by DIVINE-GRACIOUS-GRANT, not by nature. The Word, by contrast, is God by-nature (kata physin), not by-grace (kata charin); is-eternally-God, not-made-god. Athanasius pairs Exo 7:1 with Psa 82:6 (&#x27;I have said, Ye are gods&#x27;; cf. Jn 10:34) as the canonical-theōsis-by-grace pair, contrasting both with the Son&#x27;s-deity-by-essence. Crucial anti-Arian hermeneutical-grid.</p></aside>',
        "Athanasius of Alexandria, Orationes contra Arianos (Four Discourses Against the Arians, I-IV), in Select Writings and Letters of Athanasius, Nicene and Post-Nicene Fathers (NPNF), Series 2, Vol. 4, ed. Archibald Robertson (Oxford / T&T Clark, 1892). PD. Greek text in Migne PG 26 (1857).",
