        "Sabbath",
        "word",
        "Hebrew",
        "Hebrew",
        "<strong>Shabbat (<em>שַׁבָּת</em>) — to cease.</strong> The verb <em>shavat</em> means simply to stop, not “to rest” in the sense of recovery. The Sabbath commandment in Deuteronomy 5 grounds the day in liberation from slavery; here in Exodus 20 it is grounded in creation (Gen 2:2–3). The two rationales coexist throughout Israelite tradition: the day is at once cosmic and political — a weekly enactment of being a free creature in a created world.",
        "User original",
