    (
        20,
        13,
        "",
        "You shall not murder",
        "word",
        "Hebrew",
        "Hebrew",
        "<strong>Lo tirtzach (<em>לֹא</em> <em>תִרְצָח</em>).</strong> The verb <em>ratzach</em> denotes unlawful, personal killing — what English calls <em>murder</em> — not all forms of killing. The Hebrew Bible elsewhere uses different verbs (<em>harag</em>, <em>hemit</em>) for warfare and judicial execution, both of which the Torah continues to permit. Older English translations (KJV “thou shalt not kill”) blur this distinction; modern translations almost universally restore it.",
        "User original",
    ),
    # ---- expansion (May 2026) ----
    (
        2,
        2,
        "",
        "A man of the house of Levi",
        "comm",
        "Note",
        "Note",
        "<strong>Moses’ Levite genealogy.</strong> The narrative opens Moses’ story without naming his parents — Amram and Jochebed are named only later (6:20). The deliberate anonymity heightens the sense that this child arrives without context, found rather than born into rank. The detail that both parents are Levites is theologically loaded: Moses comes from the tribe that will, by the end of Exodus, be set apart for sanctuary service.",
        "User original",
    ),
    (
        7,
        11,
        "b",
        "the wise men and the sorcerers",
        "comm",
        "Note",
        "Note",
        '<strong>The Egyptian magicians.</strong> Pharaoh’s court magicians replicate the first plagues with their <em>latim</em> ("secret arts"), then fail at the gnats (8:18) and confess "this is the finger of God." Egyptian magical literature (the Demotic Magical Papyri, Coffin Texts) confirms the historical reality of the priestly-magical class the text portrays. Jewish tradition names two of them, <em>Yannes and Yambres</em> (cited in 2 Tim 3:8), expanding the narrative through pseudepigraphic literature.',
        "User original",
    ),
    (
        13,
        2,
        "",
        "Sanctify to me all the firstborn",
        "comm",
        "Note",
        "Note",
        "<strong>The firstborn.</strong> The Passover institution turns the death of the firstborn (Exod 12) into a permanent claim on Israel’s firstborn — redeemed by substitute offering (13:13) or, for the priestly tribe, by Levite substitution (Num 3:12). Luke 2:23 cites this verse describing the presentation of Jesus at the Temple. The redemption-of-firstborn ceremony (<em>pidyon ha-ben</em>) on the thirty-first day of life remains a Jewish practice descending directly from this text.",
        "User original",
    ),
    (
        15,
        1,
        "b",
        "sang this song",
        "comm-rabbinic",
        "Note",
        "Note",
        "<strong>The Song of the Sea.</strong> One of the oldest preserved poems in Hebrew, datable on linguistic grounds to the late second millennium BCE — quite possibly older than the prose narrative that introduces it. Its archaic forms (yqtl tense usage, enclitic <em>mem</em>, infrequent definite article) are diagnostic. The song closes with Yahweh enthroned forever (15:18) — possibly the earliest royal-cosmic-kingship statement in the Bible. The Mishnah (Pesachim 10) lists this song as part of the Hallel sung at Passover.",
        "User paraphrase; references Mishnah",
    ),
    (
        16,
        4,
        "",
        "I will rain bread from the sky",
        "comm",
        "Note",
        "Note",
        '<strong>Manna theology.</strong> The wilderness manna (described botanically in Num 11:7–9) is read as a sustained miracle of divine provision contingent on daily trust. Wisdom of Solomon 16:20 turns it into "food of angels"; John 6 has Jesus identifying himself as the true manna ("I am the bread of life"). The double-portion-on-Friday rule (16:5) embeds the Sabbath into the very logistics of survival — divine provisioning that itself rests.',
        "User original",
    ),
    (
        18,
        17,
        "",
        "The thing that you do is not good",
        "comm",
        "Note",
        "Note",
        "<strong>Jethro’s counsel.</strong> A non-Israelite priest of Midian gives Moses governance advice — and Moses takes it. The narrative is theologically generous: wisdom can come from outside the covenant community. The system Jethro proposes (chiefs of thousands, hundreds, fifties, tens) becomes the template for Israelite judicial administration. The Qumran community’s organizational documents (1QS) draw on this same passage for their hierarchical structure.",
        "User original",
    ),
    (
        20,
        3,
        "",
        "no other gods before me",
        "comm",
        "Note",
        "Note",
        '<strong>The first commandment.</strong> The Hebrew <em>‘al panai</em> ("before/beside my face") admits two readings: monolatry (Yahweh alone among existing gods is to be worshipped) or strict monotheism (no other gods exist). Most modern scholarship reads early Israelite religion as monolatrous, with explicit monotheism crystallizing later (Isaiah 40–55). The Decalogue’s grammar fits the monolatrous reading more naturally; the systematic monotheism becomes explicit in Deuteronomy and the prophets.',
        "User original",
    ),
