        "",
        "You shall not wrong an alien",
        "comm-contextual",
        "Note",
        "Note",
        '<strong>The alien protection laws.</strong> The motivation clause is unusual in ancient Near Eastern law: not a command from royal authority, but appeal to shared experience ("you were aliens in Egypt"). This formulation appears 36+ times in the Pentateuch — making protection of the foreigner one of the most repeated commandments in Torah. The same logic recurs in Deut 10:19 and Lev 19:33–34 ("love him as yourself"), where the foreigner becomes a paradigm-case for the love commandment.',
        "User original",
