    (
        35,
        30,
        "",
        "Bezalel the son of Uri, the son of Hur, of the tribe of Judah",
        "word",
        "Hebrew",
        "Hebrew",
        '<strong>B’tzal’el (<em>בְּצַלְאֵל</em>).</strong> "In the shadow of God" — the name encodes the chief tabernacle-craftsman’s theological status. Bezalel is the OT’s first explicitly Spirit-filled artist (31:3, 35:31, "I have filled him with the Spirit of God, with wisdom, with understanding, with knowledge, and with all manner of workmanship"). The principle the chapter introduces — that artistic craftsmanship is itself a Spirit-given gift, not a secondary skill — is one of the OT’s most distinctive theological claims about labor and creativity. The Spirit-of-Yahweh language for Bezalel uses the same vocabulary later used of prophets and judges, equating skilled craft with prophetic-charismatic giftedness. The chapter’s explicit theology of art shapes Jewish tradition (b. Berakhot 55a treats Bezalel as understanding the cosmic order through his work) and Christian reflection on artistic vocation (Dorothy Sayers, Jacques Maritain, and others draw on Bezalel as foundational text for theological aesthetics).',
        "User original",
    ),
