    (
        38,
        26,
        "",
        "a beka a head, that is, half a shekel",
        "comm",
        "Note",
        "Note",
        '<strong>The census-count by tax-receipt.</strong> The chapter reports the totals of metal collected: 100 talents and 1,775 shekels of silver, computed at half a shekel per adult male, yielding the population of fighting-age Israelite men at 603,550 (cf. Num 1:46, where the census number is identical). The verse is one of the OT’s clearest examples of administrative documentation used as historical-demographic evidence: the population is back-derived from the tax-collection. Modern critical readers are skeptical of the numbers (the 600,000+ adult males implies a total population over 2 million, demographically implausible for the wilderness scenario); various proposals (read "thousand" as "clan" or "unit" of varying size; treat as schematic-symbolic rather than literal; etc.) attempt to reduce the figure to a defensible historical claim. The verse is one of the textbook cases of OT numerical interpretation, with no consensus solution. Whatever the original numerical intent, the verse clearly intends to register monumental scale.',
        "User original",
    ),
