    (
        40,
        34,
        "",
        "Yahweh’s glory filled the tabernacle",
        "comm",
        "Note",
        "Note",
        '<strong>The glory-cloud arrives.</strong> The book’s closing scene: as soon as Moses finishes assembling the tabernacle, the cloud of Yahweh’s presence covers it and the divine glory (Hebrew <em>kavod</em>) fills the structure so densely that even Moses cannot enter. The image is of theological completion — the engineered meeting-place actually receives the deity it was designed to host. The same cloud-and-glory will later fill Solomon’s temple (1 Kgs 8:10–11) at the dedication, with the same effect (the priests cannot enter); will depart in Ezekiel’s vision (Ezek 10–11) at the temple’s defilement; and will return in Ezekiel’s eschatological temple (Ezek 43:1–5). The cloud thus traces the entire OT theology of divine presence with sanctuary across the canon. John 1:14 ("the Word became flesh and tabernacled [<em>eskēnōsen</em>] among us, and we beheld his glory") deliberately echoes this verse, claiming the incarnate Christ as the new tabernacle in which God’s glory dwells. The book of Exodus, which began with Israel groaning under Egyptian oppression (1:11), ends with Yahweh’s glory dwelling in their midst. The narrative arc is theologically complete.',
        "User original",
    ),
    (
        3,
        14,
        "m",
        "",
        "word",
        "Note",
        "Note",
        """<strong>’Ehyeh asher ’ehyeh (<em>אֶהְיֶה</em> <em>אֲשֶׁר</em> <em>אֶהְיֶה</em>).</strong> The most famous verbal puzzle in the Hebrew Bible. The verb <em>hayah</em> ("to be") is in the first-person imperfect, which can render as: "I am that I am," "I will be what I will be," "I am the one who is," "I cause to be what is." The LXX gives <em>egō eimi ho ōn</em> ("I am the One who is"), influencing all subsequent Greek-philosophical reading of God as Being itself. Maimonides took the Hebrew as denoting necessary existence; Ricoeur as fidelity-promise; Buber as presence ("I shall be present"). The form connects to the divine name YHWH (also from the root <em>h-y-h</em>).""",
        "User paraphrase; references LXX, Maimonides",
    ),
    (
        4,
        24,
        "m",
        "",
        "comm",
        "Note",
        "Note",
        """<strong>The Bridegroom of Blood.</strong> One of the strangest passages in the Pentateuch. As Moses is en route to Egypt — having just been commissioned — God attacks him at a night encampment. Zipporah saves him by circumcising her son and touching Moses’s feet with the blood, declaring "a bridegroom of blood you are to me." The episode foreshadows Passover: blood of circumcision saves Moses, blood of the lamb saves Israel.""",
        "User original",
    ),
    (
        12,
        11,
        "m",
        "",
        "comm-ethiopian",
        "Note",
        "Note",
        '<strong>Pesaḥ (<em>פֶּסַח</em>).</strong> The verb <em>pasaḥ</em>, source of the festival name, occurs in v.13 ("I will pass over") and probably means "to limp," "to skip," or "to spare." The festival meal is to be eaten standing, dressed for travel — eternalizing the urgency of the Exodus night. The Christian Eucharist takes its institution from this meal (Mark 14:12–25). The Ethiopian Tewahedo Church preserves a strong Passover-Eucharist link in its liturgy.',
        "User original",
    ),
    (
        13,
        9,
        "m",
        "",
        "comm",
        "Note",
        "Note",
        """<strong>Tefillin.</strong> Together with Deut 6:8 and 11:18, this verse is the basis for the Jewish practice of binding scriptural texts in small leather cases on the arm and head. The literal interpretation became normative; the Karaites later argued for a metaphorical reading. The phrase "a memorial between your eyes" gives the head-tefillin its place; "a sign on your hand" gives the arm-tefillin its place. Pharisaic-rabbinic literalism here vs. spiritualizing readings represents one of the deepest interpretive divergences in Second Temple Judaism.""",
        "User original",
    ),
    (
        14,
        22,
        "m",
        "",
        "comm",
        "Note",
        "Note",
        """<strong>The Sea of Reeds.</strong> The Hebrew <em>yam suf</em> is traditionally translated "Red Sea" via the LXX, but "Reed Sea" is closer to the Hebrew. Likely refers to a marshy lake region in the eastern Nile delta. The miracle is described differently in different sources within the chapter: a strong east wind drying the sea (14:21 — naturalistic), water as walls (14:22 — supernatural). Both readings are kept side by side in the canonical text.""",
        "User paraphrase; references LXX",
    ),
    (
        15,
        3,
        "m",
        "",
        "comm-ethiopian",
        "Note",
        "Note",
        "<strong>The Song of the Sea.</strong> Exodus 15 is one of the oldest poetic strata of the Hebrew Bible (alongside Judges 5, the Song of Deborah). Its archaic Hebrew, parallelism, and triumphal celebration of God-as-warrior preserves a martial theology that later prophetic and wisdom traditions will both honor and complicate. The Ethiopian Tewahedo liturgy still incorporates this song. The image of God as warrior is uncomfortable for many modern readers; the Bible itself wrestles with it (cf. Isa 2:4, the swords-into-plowshares).",
        "User original",
    ),
    (
        17,
        11,
        "m",
        "",
        "comm-ethiopian",
        "Note",
        "Note",
        "<strong>The lifted hands.</strong> Moses’s upraised arms — held up by Aaron and Hur — became one of early Christianity’s most-cited typologies of the Cross (already in the Epistle of Barnabas 12; later in Justin, Tertullian, Augustine). The Ethiopian Tewahedo iconographic tradition preserves the same reading. Independent of the typology, the narrative is clear: the battle’s fortunes follow the prayer-posture, not the military skill.",
        "User paraphrase; references Tertullian, Augustine",
    ),
    (
        19,
        6,
        "m",
        "",
        "comm",
        "Note",
        "Note",
        """<strong>Mamleket kohanim (<em>מַמְלֶכֶת</em> <em>כֹּהֲנִים</em>).</strong> The corporate priesthood of Israel — every member a priest before the nations. 1 Peter 2:9 applies the same phrase to the Church. The democratization of priesthood is one of Israel’s distinctive contributions to ancient religious thought; in surrounding ANE cultures, priesthood was a royal-administrative class. Israel inverts: the whole people is mediator.""",
        "User original",
    ),
    (
        20,
        3,
        "m",
        "",
        "comm",
        "Note",
        "Note",
        """<strong>The First Commandment.</strong> "Before me" (<em>‘al panay</em>) more literally "in my presence" or "to my face." Some scholars distinguish monolatry (worshipping one God while granting others exist) from monotheism (denying others exist) — and read this commandment as monolatry, with monotheism developing later (Deut 4:35, Isa 44:6). The classical Jewish and Christian reading, however, takes the commandment as already implicitly monotheistic.""",
        "User original",
    ),
    (
        24,
        10,
        "m",
        "",
        "comm",
        "Note",
        "Note",
        """<strong>The covenant meal.</strong> One of the most extraordinary passages in scripture: the seventy elders "see God" and eat with him on the mountain. The text immediately notes God did not lay a hand on them — i.e., they survived the encounter. The pavement of sapphire under God’s feet later becomes a stock image in apocalyptic literature (Ezek 1:26). The communal meal in God’s presence prefigures Eucharistic theology.""",
        "User original",
    ),
    (
        25,
        8,
        "m",
        "",
        "word",
        "Note",
        "Note",
        """<strong>Shakanti (<em>שָׁכַנְתִּי</em>) — "I will dwell."</strong> The verb gives us <em>mishkan</em>, "tabernacle" (the dwelling), and later <em>Shekhinah</em> — the rabbinic/mystical term for God’s indwelling presence. John 1:14 echoes this when he says the Logos "tabernacled (<em>eskēnōsen</em>) among us" — the Greek echoes the same root. The whole tabernacle theology of Exodus is reread Christologically through this single verb.""",
        "User original",
    ),
    (
        32,
        32,
        "m",
        "",
        "comm",
        "Note",
        "Note",
        """<strong>Moses’s intercession.</strong> Moses offers to be erased from God’s book in place of Israel — one of the most striking acts of intercessory love in scripture. Paul’s parallel in Romans 9:3 ("I could wish myself accursed for my brothers") explicitly invokes this. "The book" is the heavenly register of the righteous, picked up in Ps 69:28, Dan 12:1, Phil 4:3, Rev 3:5.""",
        "User original",
    ),
    (
        33,
        19,
        "m",
        "",
        "comm-rabbinic",
        "Note",
        "Note",
        '<strong>The hiddenness of God.</strong> The face/back distinction — Moses sees God’s "back" but not his face — frames a paradox of revelation. God is fully self-disclosing yet remains beyond comprehension. John 1:18 ("no one has seen God at any time; the only-begotten Son… has made him known") echoes the verse explicitly. The medieval Jewish tradition of <em>negative theology</em> (Maimonides) draws heavily on this passage.',
        "User paraphrase; references Maimonides",
    ),
    (
        34,
        6,
        "m",
        "",
        "comm",
        "Note",
        "Note",
        """<strong>The Thirteen Attributes.</strong> Exodus 34:6–7, recited in Jewish liturgy on fasts and high holy days, is the Hebrew Bible’s most concentrated theological self-description of God. Echoes recur all through the canon (Num 14:18, Joel 2:13, Jonah 4:2, Ps 86:15, 103:8, 145:8, Neh 9:17). The list is the closest the Hebrew Bible comes to a creedal statement about God’s nature.""",
        "User original",
    ),
